TTYCHATTER.PYTHON(1)        User Commands        TTYCHATTER.PYTHON(1)

NAME
    ttychatter.python - Bash terminal client with Python 3 helper routines

SYNOPSIS
    ttychatter.python [session]
    ttychatter.python --resume <session|session.log|/path/session.log>
    ttychatter.python --models [--all] [--model-type TYPE]
    ttychatter.python --routers
    ttychatter.python --update-models
    ttychatter.python --test-model <model_id>
    ttychatter.python --select-model
    ttychatter.python --doctor
    ttychatter.python --demo
    ttychatter.python --version
    ttychatter.python --loopback
    ttychatter.python --loopback-file <path>
    ttychatter.python --help

DESCRIPTION
    ttychatter.python is the full line-oriented terminal client for
    ttychatter. Bash orchestrates the user workflow and Python 3 helper snippets
    handle JSON, URL/path handling, attachment extraction, and log parsing.

ROUTERS AND FIXED MODELS
    OpenRouter model catalogs contain fixed model IDs and router-like entries.
    Use these commands to inspect model types:

        ttychatter.python --routers
        ttychatter.python --models --model-type routers
        ttychatter.python --models --model-type fixed
        ttychatter.python --models --model-type free
        ttychatter.python --models --model-type auto

RUNTIME COMMAND MODE
    During interactive chat, inputs beginning with ':' are local commands rather
    than messages to OpenRouter. Examples:
        :help
        :models
        :routers
        :select-model
        :test-model openrouter/auto
        :set CONTEXT_TURNS 20
        :memory
        :attach ./notes.txt
        :credits
        :quit

LOOPBACK OUTPUT
    ttychatter.python --loopback
        Sends interface text to stderr and clean AI responses to stdout so the
        output can be piped into another program.

DEMO MODE
    --demo provides local model rows and local AI responses. It is useful for
    testing sessions, command mode, and attachment extraction without making
    OpenRouter chat requests.

CONFIGURATION
    Config file:
        ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/config

    Model-related keys include MODEL, MODEL_TYPE_FILTER, MODEL_FAVORITES_FILE, THEME, SEND_INPUT, CODE_ATTACHMENT_MIN_LINES, MODEL_SORT_ORDER,
    MODEL_FILTER_REQUIRE_TOKENS, MODEL_FILTER_HIDE_PREVIEW, and token-limit
    filters.  Supported MODEL_TYPE_FILTER, MODEL_FAVORITES_FILE, THEME, SEND_INPUT, CODE_ATTACHMENT_MIN_LINES values are all, routers, fixed, free,
    and auto.


MODEL FAVORITES
    Model favorites/bookmarks are stored as plain text and are shared with
    model-list sorting/filtering where the client supports it.

    Commands:
        ttychatter.python --favorites
        ttychatter.python --favorite-model <model_id>
        ttychatter.python --unfavorite-model <model_id>

    Runtime commands inside chat:
        :favorites
        :favorite <model_id>
        :unfavorite <model_id>

DISPLAY AND SEND CONFIGURATION
    THEME controls the line-oriented display profile where meaningful.
    Supported values include default, dim, high_contrast, mono, and
    solarized_dark. Bash clients only emit ANSI styling when stdout is a
    terminal; loopback/pipeline output remains plain text.

    SEND_INPUT controls the interactive input loop.  ctrl_d keeps the classic
    multiline Bash behavior.  enter reads one line per message.  ctrl_enter is
    terminal-dependent and is not reliable in line-oriented Bash clients.

CODE BLOCK ATTACHMENT THRESHOLD
    CODE_ATTACHMENT_MIN_LINES controls when generated fenced code blocks are
    saved as attachments.  Shorter blocks remain inline so ordinary short code
    examples do not disappear from the transcript.

VERSION
    ttychatter.python version 3.9
